---
/**
 * LeadForm — the company's live GoHighLevel lead form, embedded.
 *
 * ONE COMPONENT, EVERY PAGE. BaseLayout renders it at the foot of <main> on
 * every page (owner's call, 11 Sep 2026 — see the leadForm prop there and
 * QuickNav.astro, whose button at the top of the page jumps here), so the form
 * reaches every page from a single edit. That is deliberate and it is the
 * same call LocationMap makes: an iframe pasted into each template is the
 * duplication this codebase has had to undo three times already. If the
 * form id ever changes it changes in
 * src/data/business.ts and nowhere else.
 *
 * COPIED, NOT REBUILT. Every attribute below is transcribed from the embed on
 * the live WordPress site. The reasoning for embedding rather than building a
 * native form lives on `business.crmForm` and is not repeated here.
 *
 * WHY THE ROOT IS AN <aside> AND MUST STAY ONE. The harness strips
 * data-boilerplate blocks with
 *     /<([a-z]+)[^>]*\sdata-boilerplate\b[^>]*>[\s\S]*?<\/\1>/gi
 * which backreferences the tag name but is still NON-GREEDY — it stops at the
 * first closing tag of that name. Cta.astro's root is <section
 * data-boilerplate>; if this component's root were a <section>, the strip on
 * the CTA would end at OUR closing tag and leak the rest of the CTA into both
 * the word count and the duplicate-sentence scanner, on 207 pages at once.
 * <aside> is a tag neither wrapper uses, so both blocks strip whole. For the
 * same reason: never nest an <aside> inside this one.
 *
 * NOT CHECKED BY THE HARNESS. Check 1b crawls img/script/link/meta and skips
 * external URLs outright — "someone else's" — so a typo in the host or the
 * form id fails silently in production rather than in the gate. Both were
 * verified by fetching them: the widget returns 200 text/html and
 * form_embed.js returns 200 and 34KB. Re-verify by hand if either is edited.
 */
import { business } from '../data/business';
import {
  LEAD_FORM_ID,
  freeInspectionForPath,
  offerLabel,
  langForPath,
} from '../lib/offer';

interface Props {
  /** The visible heading. Kept a prop so the contact page can lead with it. */
  heading?: string;
  /** Contact page renders the form as the point of the page, not as a footnote. */
  primary?: boolean;
  /**
   * Start the form as soon as the page has loaded, without waiting for the
   * reader.
   *
   * UNUSED BY DEFAULT, and kept as the owner's switch. It was written for the
   * contact pages, where the form IS the page. Keystone v2's lab gate then
   * measured the choice both ways on 10 Sep 2026: with `eager`, /contact/ sat
   * at an LCP of 3.08s (median of five) and failed; with the ordinary rule —
   * the form starts on the reader's first tap or scroll, or 2.5s after load —
   * it passed at 1.86s. v2 says a page that fails the lab gate is not
   * published, so the contact pages use the ordinary rule. Adding `eager`
   * back to <LeadForm /> on contact.astro is a one-word change if the owner
   * decides the instant form is worth the failed gate.
   */
  eager?: boolean;
}
/* THE DEFAULT HEADING IS COMPUTED, NOT TYPED, and the bug that made it so is
   worth recording: it shipped as a hard-coded "Request a free estimate" and
   every page then carried a form headed "estimate" beneath a CTA button and a
   sticky bar both offering an inspection. Three controls, one offer, two
   different nouns. The rule lives in src/lib/offer.ts and all three read it.

   Astro.url.pathname rather than a prop, because this component is rendered
   from twenty route files and a prop is twenty chances to forget one — which
   is how the wording drifted in the first place. Paths carry a trailing slash
   (vercel.json sets trailingSlash), which is the shape freeInspectionForPath
   expects. */
const lang = langForPath(Astro.url.pathname);
const {
  heading = offerLabel(freeInspectionForPath(Astro.url.pathname), lang),
  primary = false,
  eager = false,
} = Astro.props;

const f = business.crmForm;
const src = `${f.host}/widget/form/${f.id}`;
const frameId = `inline-${f.id}`;

/* WORDING. "Estimate", never "inspection" — the same rule Cta.astro spends a
   paragraph on, and it applies harder here because this heading renders on
   every page. GHL's own name for the form is "GET A FREE ESTIMATE", so the
   heading and the form agree, and harness check 2c stays quiet. */
---
{/* id is the sticky action bar's jump target, and it is on the OUTER element
    so the reader lands on the heading rather than mid-form. There is exactly
    one form per page — the GHL loader finds its frame by id, so a second copy
    would break both — which is what makes a bare id safe here. §3's
    [id] { scroll-margin-top } keeps the landing clear of the sticky top bar. */}
<aside
  id={LEAD_FORM_ID}
  class:list={['leadform', primary && 'is-primary']}
  data-boilerplate
>
  <h2>{heading}</h2>

  {/* THE RESERVED BOX. min-height rather than the aspect-ratio box the map
      uses, because a form is not a fixed rectangle: GHL's form_embed.js
      measures the real content and sets an inline height, which would fight
      an absolutely-positioned frame. Reserving GHL's own declared height
      means the common case lands with no shift, and the frame is free to grow
      for validation messages without being clipped.

      THE FRAME LOADS WHEN THE READER NEARS IT — changed 10 Sep 2026, and the
      note that stood here until then is worth keeping because it was right to
      refuse this without evidence:

        "NO loading="lazy" HERE… form_embed.js is a third-party loader that
        looks the frame up by id and wires a resize postMessage; whether it
        tolerates a frame that has not fetched yet is not something this
        project can test… Revisit only with a real browser in front of you."

      Keystone v2 put a real browser in front of it. Its lab gate (median of
      five mobile Lighthouse runs) measured every template at an LCP of 8–11
      seconds against a 2.0s budget, and the eager embed was the bulk of it:
      each page pulled about 2.5MB it never showed — four Cloudflare Turnstile
      challenge payloads of ~420KB each, GHL's own 261KB bundle and Facebook's
      tracking pixel — for a form most readers never scroll to.

      What changed is WHEN, not HOW. The loader's order of operations is kept
      exactly: the frame gets its src first, then form_embed.js is added, so
      the script still finds a frame that is already fetching — the same
      sequence it met when both were in the HTML. Both now start on the
      reader's first interaction, when the form enters the viewport, or five
      seconds after load — the script below has the reasons and
      the two versions that came before it. On the contact pages the form is
      on the first screen and loads straight away. Verified in headless Chrome
      by scripts/check-leadform.mjs on the day: nothing from the form host
      loads during the first paint, GHL's form renders inside the frame once
      the reader scrolls, and the resize handshake sets its height. Re-run it
      after touching this. */}
  <div class="leadform-frame">
    <iframe
      data-src={src}
      style="width:100%;height:100%;border:none;border-radius:4px"
      id={frameId}
      data-layout="{'id':'INLINE'}"
      data-trigger-type="alwaysShow"
      data-trigger-value=""
      data-activation-type="alwaysActivated"
      data-activation-value=""
      data-deactivation-type="neverDeactivate"
      data-deactivation-value=""
      data-form-name={f.name}
      data-height={String(f.height)}
      data-layout-iframe-id={frameId}
      data-form-id={f.id}
      title={lang === 'es' ? `${f.name} (formulario en inglés)` : f.name}
    ></iframe>
  </div>

  {/* THE FLOOR UNDER THE EMBED, and the same argument LocationMap makes for
      its plain Google Maps link. A third-party frame can be blocked by an
      extension, a strict privacy mode or a CRM outage, and when it is, the
      block degrades to a heading and an empty rectangle — on a page whose job
      is to collect a lead. The phone number is the real fallback here: it is
      how most of this company's work arrives anyway, and it needs nothing to
      load. The direct form link is there for anyone who would rather type. */}
  {/* THE SPANISH LINE SAYS THE FORM IS IN ENGLISH, because it is: the fields
      live inside the CRM's own frame and this site cannot translate them.
      Saying so beside the phone number — and that we speak Spanish on it —
      is the honest version until the owner has a Spanish form built in the
      CRM (recorded in scripts/pending.mjs). */}
  {lang === 'es' ? (
    <p class="leadform-fallback">
      El formulario está en inglés. Si prefiere, llame o mande un mensaje de
      texto al <a href={`tel:${business.phoneE164}`}>{business.phone}</a> —
      hablamos español — o{' '}
      <a href={src} rel="noopener">abra el formulario en su propia página</a>.
    </p>
  ) : (
    <p class="leadform-fallback">
      Form not loading? Call or text{' '}
      <a href={`tel:${business.phoneE164}`}>{business.phone}</a>, or{' '}
      <a href={src} rel="noopener">open the form on its own page</a>.
    </p>
  )}
</aside>

{/* The loader. Inline and tiny, so it costs nothing on the pages where the
    reader never reaches the form. It sets the frame's src and only then adds
    GHL's form_embed.js — byte-for-byte as GHL serves it, never bundled — which
    is the order the two had when both sat in the HTML. One form per page, so
    one frame id and one copy of this. Without IntersectionObserver (very old
    browsers) it starts at once, which is the old behavior. Without JavaScript
    the frame stays empty and the fallback line under it — phone number and a
    direct link to the form — is what the reader gets, as it always was when
    the embed failed. */}
<script is:inline define:vars={{ frameId, eager, embed: `${business.crmForm.host}/js/form_embed.js` }}>
  (function () {
    var frame = document.getElementById(frameId);
    if (!frame) return;
    var started = false;
    function start() {
      if (started) return;
      started = true;
      frame.src = frame.getAttribute('data-src');
      var s = document.createElement('script');
      s.src = embed;
      s.defer = true;
      document.body.appendChild(s);
    }
    /* Whichever comes first:
         · the form enters the viewport (a jump to #lead-form from the sticky
           bar, or a page where it is on the first screen). No margin: the
           form sits about 1,035px down on a phone, and a 200px margin on an
           844px screen reached it on arrival, which loaded it at once;
         · the reader does anything at all — scrolls, taps, presses a key —
           which on a page with the form one screen down is nearly always the
           start of the journey to it, and gives the frame a head start;
         · five seconds after the page's load event, so a reader who simply
           waits still finds it ready.
       The second version of this started on load + idle. The lab showed why
       that was not enough: on a fast connection "idle" arrives about 400ms in,
       inside the window where the page itself is still painting, and GHL's
       2MB then competed with the first paint on every English template — they
       sat at 3.1s while the Spanish pages, which carry no form, painted in
       1.4s. Waiting for the reader moves those bytes out of that window on a
       real slow phone as well as in the lab. */
    /* A FOURTH VERSION, for the pages where the form is ON the first screen.
       Imported blog posts carry no Quick Answer, so the form sits directly
       under the H1 — and "enters the viewport" fired on arrival, loading GHL
       during the H1's own paint. The lab put those posts at 3.1s against 1.9s
       everywhere else. So being visible is no longer enough on its own: a
       form that is on screen at arrival waits until 2.5s after the load event
       unless the reader does something first. On a page where the reader has
       to scroll to reach it nothing changes, because the scroll itself is the
       trigger. The contact pages opt out with `eager`. */
    /* eager: straight after the page's own load event — not during parse.
       Starting it the moment this script ran put the contact page at 3.1s
       in the lab; after load, the page paints first and the form follows at
       once, which is the order a reader on that page wants anyway. */
    if (eager) {
      if (document.readyState === 'complete') start();
      else window.addEventListener('load', start);
      return;
    }
    var kick = function () { start(); };
    ['scroll', 'pointerdown', 'keydown', 'touchstart'].forEach(function (ev) {
      window.addEventListener(ev, kick, { once: true, passive: true });
    });
    var loaded = false;
    window.addEventListener('load', function () {
      loaded = true;
      setTimeout(start, 5000);
    });
    if (!('IntersectionObserver' in window)) return;
    var io = new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
      for (var i = 0; i < entries.length; i++) {
        if (!entries[i].isIntersecting) continue;
        io.disconnect();
        if (loaded) setTimeout(start, 2500);
        else window.addEventListener('load', function () { setTimeout(start, 2500); });
        return;
      }
    }, { rootMargin: '0px' });
    io.observe(frame);
  })();
</script>
